  "Question": "An athlete’s target heart rate, in beats per minute, is \\(80\\%\\) of the theoretical maximum heart rate. The maximum heart rate is found by subtracting the athlete’s age, in years, from \\(220.\\) To the nearest whole number, what is the target heart rate of an athlete who is \\(26\\) years old?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(134\\)",
    "B": "\\(155\\)",
    "C": "\\(176\\)",
    "D": "\\(194\\)",
    "E": "\\(243\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "First find the maximum heart rate, because the target is a percentage of that number. For a \\(26\\)-year-old athlete, the maximum is\n\\[\n220-26=194\n\\]\nbeats per minute. Since \\(80\\%=0.8\\), the target is\n\\[\n0.8\\times194=155.2\n\\]\nbeats per minute. The tenths digit is \\(2\\), so rounding to the nearest whole number gives \\(155\\) beats per minute, choice B.",
